On 11/28/2013 03:38 AM, Mark Seeto wrote:
> Dear lavaan group,
>
> The help page for the sem function says that '|"MLR"| will compute a
> test statistic which is asymptotically equivalent to the Yuan-Bentler
> T2-star test statistic', from which I infer that the MLR test statistic
> is not the same as the Yuan-Bentler T2-star test statistic. My question
> then is what is the MLR test statistic? Is the equation written down in
> an article somewhere?

Not in an article (that I know about). But I once gave a presentation
explaining the difference:

http://users.ugent.be/~yrosseel/lavaan/utrecht2010.pdf

(see slide 22).

Now, lavaan currently uses the 'Mplus' approach by default. The reason
is that I know how to handle 'fixed exogenous covariates' in this
setting, but I have not figured out how to do this for the original
Yuan-Bentler formulas (they assumed fixed.x=FALSE).

To get the original Yuan-Bentler formula, use mimic="EQS" (which will
imply fixed.x=FALSE)

In the source code (see file 08lavaanTest.R), we have two functions:

testStatisticYuanBentler
testStatisticYuanBentler.Mplus

implementing the 'two' versions.

Yves.

From u89c9fc822e34b78c at groups.google.com  Mon Dec  2 12:45:43 2013
From: u89c9fc822e34b78c at groups.google.com (yrosseel)
Date: Mon, 02 Dec 2013 12:45:43 +0000
Subject: Re: models with identical coefficients producing radically different R^2 values
Message-ID: <VrPYcTb_194.hdLuhn6TJt8J@lavaan.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <VrPYcTb_194.h4ig0xhpbAoJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
References: <VrPYcTb_194.h4ig0xhpbAoJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/VrPYcTb_194/m/hdLuhn6TJt8J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The confusion seems to stem from the formula that is used to compute the
R^2.

R^2 of a dependent variable (say, y2), is 1 minus the (estimated)
standardized residual variance for y2. In our example:

set.seed(2002)
adf <- data.frame(x=rnorm(100), x1=rnorm(100), y1=rnorm(100), y2=rnorm(100))

mod1 <- ' y2 ~ x + x1 + y1 '
mod2 <- ' y2 ~ x + x1 + y1; y1 ~ x1 '

fit1 <- sem(mod1, data=adf)
fit2 <- sem(mod2, data=adf)
summary(fit1, standardized=TRUE)
summary(fit2, standardized=TRUE)

For fit1, we get (see last column):

                    Estimate  Std.err  Z-value  P(>|z|)   Std.lv  Std.all

Variances:
     y2                0.743    0.105                      0.743    0.935

while for fit2, we get

                    Estimate  Std.err  Z-value  P(>|z|)   Std.lv  Std.all

Variances:
     y2                0.743    0.105                      0.743    0.923

So we need to explain how we compute the 'Std.all' value for y2 in both
settings.

We need to compute the model-implied variances for each model, and this
is where we see the differences (see first element 'y2'):

 > diag( fitted(fit1)$cov )
        y2         x        x1        y1
0.7948851 1.0103473 0.9897522 0.8568083

 > diag( fitted(fit2)$cov )
        y2        y1         x        x1
0.8051950 0.8568084 1.0103473 0.9897522

Dividing the original estimate (0.743) by either 0.7948851 or 0.8051950
gives the two Std.all versions.

Why are the fitted variances different? Because in mod2, we have a
non-zero regression coefficient in the beta matrix (for y2 ~ x1), and
this affects the corresponding element in 'psi' matrix, and evenutally
the model implied (co)variances. Since we have no latent variables, the
model-implied covariance matrix is given by

solve(diag(4)-beta) %*% psi %*% t(  solve(diag(4)-beta) )

Both beta and psi are a bit different in the two models. but it is the
psi matrix that makes the difference.

 > inspect(fit1, "est")[3:4]
$psi
    y2     x      x1     y1
y2  0.743
x   0.000  1.010
x1  0.000 -0.035  0.990
y1  0.000  0.154  0.017  0.857

$beta
    y2     x     x1     y1
y2  0 0.181 -0.001 -0.184
x   0 0.000  0.000  0.000
x1  0 0.000  0.000  0.000
y1  0 0.000  0.000  0.000

 > inspect(fit2, "est")[3:4]
$psi
    y2     y1     x      x1
y2  0.743
y1  0.000  0.857
x   0.000  0.000  1.010
x1  0.000  0.000 -0.035  0.990

$beta
    y2     y1     x     x1
y2  0 -0.184 0.181 -0.001
y1  0  0.000 0.000  0.017
x   0  0.000 0.000  0.000
x1  0  0.000 0.000  0.000

Yves.

