Message-ID: <zEVjQogS1j8.CUXlTIXF6pEJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <zEVjQogS1j8.bNszEtWyZqgJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
References: <zEVjQogS1j8.H1v6o_q4n3oJ@lavaan.groups.google.com> <zEVjQogS1j8.bNszEtWyZqgJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/zEVjQogS1j8/m/CUXlTIXF6pEJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

You have the same problem, since Y is dependent on M.  Correlation of this disturbance with the predictor M is a violation of a major least squares regression assumption.  Moreover, think of the covariance matrix of X, M, Y.  It has 6 unique elements.  Your unknowns are #1 variance of X, # 2- #3 residual variances of M and Y, #4 #5 #6 the three regression paths.  That's all you can estimate.
--Ed Rigdon

Sent from my iPad

