I agree with that line of reasoning.  However, one can often obtain models that fit the data equally well, in which case predictive inference *does* matter. Or, as is the situation I'm running into, models that produce excessively high R^2 values with very little predictive power from the coefficients.

Different tests for different purposes.  You're completely right to maintain both.  For now, if you'd like, feel free to include the function I've provided as an auxilliary function until you can fold it in more tightly.

